BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/hls/event/2486312-ai-agents-working-for-you-or-working-against-you"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:AI Agents: Working for You or Working Against You?
DTSTART:20261201T210000Z
DTEND:20261201T223000Z
DTSTAMP:20261116T135911Z
UID:20261201T210000Z-2486312@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261201T210000Z
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;  &#160\;A
 BOUT"Trust\, but Verify: Living with AI Agents"Consumer AI agents promise 
 better deals\, cleaner inboxes\, and relief from the steady enshittificati
 on of apps and platforms. Many expect them to shift power toward buyers\, 
 and sellers seem to agree: Amazon and\, more recently\, United Airlines ha
 ve pushed back rather than cede any control of their platforms.Beneath the
  promise\, though\, lie hard questions of trust. How do we know our agents
  are finding the best deals\, or that they understand what we want when we
  often don't know ourselves? And how do we know they aren't manipulating u
 s\, whether for the companies that built them or for sellers who use promp
 t injection to turn them into double agents?Drawing on Elinor Ostrom's wor
 k on governing shared resources\, this talk asks how buyers\, firms\, and 
 regulators can sustain trust in agent-driven global markets. The answer is
 n't to stop building agents. Buyers will still need to beware\, just of di
 fferent things.Interested in AI\, digital privacy\, consumer power\, or th
 e future of technology? Join the conversation!WHEN:Tuesday\, December 1st 
 from 4:00-5:30 pm  WHERE:IU Student Building\, Room 150  BIOGRAPHYChris Sa
 vagehas spent forty years practicing law at the point where new technology
  outruns old rules. From the breakup of the phone monopoly through the com
 mercial Internet\, online privacy\, and now artificial intelligence. He is
  a partner in the Technology\, Communications\, Privacy and Security group
  at Davis Wright Tremaine\, and teaches AI Law\, Cyberlaw\, and Informatio
 n Privacy Law at Catholic University's Columbus School of Law. A Harvard L
 aw School graduate\, he is the author of "Managing the Ambient Trust Commo
 ns" (Stanford Technology Law Review)\, which draws on Elinor Ostrom's work
  to treat online privacy as a common-pool resource problem. His current re
 search asks what happens to contracts\, commerce\, and trust when parties 
 to a deal are AI agents. Meeting URL: https://iu.zoom.us/meeting/register/
 ECvwJiP6RJKH4R_BIB7-qg
GEO:39.167135;-86.52557
LAST-MODIFIED:20261117T195445Z
LOCATION:701 E Kirkwood Ave\, Bloomington\, IN 47405
URL:https://events.iu.edu/hls/event/2486312-ai-agents-working-for-you-or-w
 orking-against-you
