X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Arts & Humanities Institute": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2463756-clacs-faculty-research-symposium-with-luciana", "IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2463756-clacs-faculty-research-symposium-with-luciana"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2463756-clacs-faculty-research-symposium-with-luciana"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:CLACS Faculty Research Symposium with Luciana Namorato
DTSTART:20261203T210000Z
DTEND:20261203T220000Z
DTSTAMP:20261103T094733Z
UID:20261203T210000Z-2471899@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261203T210000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/137/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,2013,2013/33266_Namorato1.rev.1794617124.jpg
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:The Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) we
 lcomes Dr. Luciana Namorato to present\, When a Reflex Becomes the Key: Wh
 at Women Artists Do with the Ways They Are Read\, as&#160\;part of the Fal
 l 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.Thursday\, December 3rd\, 4-5PM at
  the Hamilton Lugar School (355 N Eagleson Ave.) GA1060Abstract:  Why do w
 e so often explain a woman artist's work by her life? Editing two books on
  Latin American women artists\, I saw this become a reflex. For Latin Amer
 ican women\, two more reflexes follow: they are read through labels\, and 
 their work ends up classed as "craft." My research asks how women artists 
 have answered these reflexes\, often turning them into opportunities\, as 
 Frida Kahlo did with her life story. What shuts doors as a reflex may open
  them as a key. This talk turns to two Brazilian artists\, the writer Amar
 a Moira and the poet and dancer Gal Freire\, for whom the labels accumulat
 e ("woman\," "Latin American woman\," "transgender"). By working through t
 he reflexes that confine how they are read\, Moira and Freire make their w
 ork a fruitful space for questions that concern every artist: life and wor
 k\, the particular and the universal\, art and craft.Bio:Luciana Namorato\
 , Associate Professor in the Department of Spanish and Portuguese at IUB\,
  is co-editor of The Other Fridas: The Lives and Works of Latin American W
 omen Artists (2025) and Da margem à palavra: Escritoras brasileiras do Oi
 tocentos à atualidade (2026). Her research takes a transatlantic approach
  to Luso-Brazilian and Latin American literatures\, with particular attent
 ion to the relationships among literature\, gender\, and ethnic and nation
 al identities. She holds a Ph.D. from the University of North Carolina at 
 Chapel Hill and\, as a Fulbright fellow\, conducted postdoctoral research 
 at the University of Coimbra\, Portugal.  &#160\;
LAST-MODIFIED:20261120T165700Z
LOCATION:Hamilton Lugar School
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2463756-clacs-faculty-research-sympo
 sium-with-luciana
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">  The Center for La
 tin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) welcomes Dr. <strong>Luciana Na
 morato</strong> to present\, <em><strong>When a Reflex Becomes the Key: Wh
 at Women Artists Do with the Ways They Are Read\,</strong></em> as&#160\;p
 art of the Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.</p><p data-pm-slice
 ="1 1 []"><strong>Thursday\, December 3rd</strong>\, <strong>4-5PM</strong
 > at the <strong>Hamilton Lugar School</strong> (355 N Eagleson Ave.) <str
 ong>GA1060</strong></p><p><strong>Abstract:</strong></p><p>  Why do we so 
 often explain a woman artist's work by her life? Editing two books on Lati
 n American women artists\, I saw this become a reflex. For Latin American 
 women\, two more reflexes follow: they are read through labels\, and their
  work ends up classed as "craft." My research asks how women artists have 
 answered these reflexes\, often turning them into opportunities\, as Frida
  Kahlo did with her life story. What shuts doors as a reflex may open them
  as a key. This talk turns to two Brazilian artists\, the writer <strong>A
 mara Moira</strong> and the poet and dancer <strong>Gal Freire</strong>\, 
 for whom the labels accumulate ("woman\," "Latin American woman\," "transg
 ender"). By working through the reflexes that confine how they are read\, 
 Moira and Freire make their work a fruitful space for questions that conce
 rn every artist: life and work\, the particular and the universal\, art an
 d craft.</p><p><span style="font-style: inherit\; font-weight: inherit\;">
 <strong>Bio:</strong></span></p><p><span style="font-style: inherit\; font
 -weight: inherit\;"><strong>Luciana Namorato</strong>\, Associate Professo
 r in the Department of Spanish and Portuguese at IUB\, is co-editor of <em
 >T<strong>he Other Fridas: The Lives and Works of Latin American Women Art
 ists</strong></em> (2025) and <strong><em>Da margem à palavra: Escritoras
  brasileiras do Oitocentos à atualidade</em></strong> (2026). Her researc
 h takes a transatlantic approach to Luso-Brazilian and Latin American lite
 ratures\, with particular attention to the relationships among literature\
 , gender\, and ethnic and national identities. She holds a Ph.D. from the 
 University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill and\, as a Fulbright fellow\, 
 conducted postdoctoral research at the University of Coimbra\, Portugal.</
 span></p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><picture class="lw_image lw_image33629  lw_ali
 gn_left"><source media="(max-width: 500px)" type="image/webp" srcset="http
