X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/cahi/event/2490144-ai-and-humanities-research-risk-opportunity-and"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:AI and Humanities Research: Risk\, Opportunity\, and Change — A 
 Talk by Robert Townsend
DTSTART:20261204T180000Z
DTEND:20261204T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20261119T173050Z
UID:20261204T180000Z-2490144@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261204T180000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/130/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,600,600/33621_townsend-sq.rev.1795126053.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Drawing on recent experiments with AI for the Humanities Indic
 ators and a study of historians' adoption of earlier technologies\, Townse
 nd will discuss AI's strengths and limitations in humanities research\, ho
 w it might redraw the boundary between assistance and expertise in the fie
 ld\, and why these experiments have nevertheless made him more optimistic 
 about the future of the humanities.  This talk is co-sponsored by the Alli
 ance of Distinguished and Titled Professors (https://alliance.iu.edu/).  L
 ater that afternoon\, Townsend will also give the inaugural lecture in a n
 ew series on the current landscape both for the humanities and for humanit
 ies funding:What's Really Happening to the Humanities?(And What You Can Do
  About It)  4:00 pm\, Bridgwaters Lounge\, Neal-Marshall Black Culture Cen
 ter  275 N. Eagleson Avenue\, Bloomington\, Indiana  The decline in humani
 ties majors is a familiar story\, but what explains it? And should that be
  the principal measure of success for the field? Drawing on more than a de
 cade of research by the Humanities Indicators\, Townsend will offer a wide
 -ranging look at the state of the humanities\, examine the pressures shapi
 ng our future\, and suggest ways faculty and institutions might respond.Ro
 bert Townsend oversees Humanities\, Arts\, and Culture programs and day-to
 -day work on the Humanities Indicators (https://www.amacad.org/humanities-
 indicators) at the American Academy of Arts and Sciences (https://www.amac
 ad.org/). Prior to the Academy\, he spent 24 years at the American Histori
 cal Association\, in positions ranging from editorial assistant to deputy 
 director. He is the author of History's Babel: Scholarship\, Professionali
 zation\, and the Historical Enterprise in the United States\, 1880-1940 (U
 niversity of Chicago Press\, 2013) and author or co-author of over 200 art
 icles on various aspects of history\, higher education\, and public humani
 ties.  These talks are presented by the College Arts + Humanities Institut
 e with generous assistance from the College of Arts + Sciences and the Rut
 h N. Halls Humanities Fund. For more information\, please visit cahi.india
 na.edu (https://cahi.indiana.edu). If need assistance or if you have an ac
 commodation request\, please email cahi@iu.edu (mailto:cahi@iu.edu) or cal
 l 812-856-1169.
GEO:39.16829;-86.516866
LAST-MODIFIED:20261119T223222Z
LOCATION:Neal-Marshall Black Culture Center
URL:https://events.iu.edu/cahi/event/2490144-ai-and-humanities-research-ri
 sk-opportunity-and
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Drawing on recent experiments with AI fo
 r the Humanities Indicators and a study of historians' adoption of earlier
  technologies\, Townsend will discuss AI's strengths and limitations in hu
 manities research\, how it might redraw the boundary between assistance an
 d expertise in the field\, and why these experiments have nevertheless mad
 e him more optimistic about the future of the humanities.</p><p>  This tal
 k is co-sponsored by the <a href="https://alliance.iu.edu/" target="_blank
 " rel="noopener">Alliance of Distinguished and Titled Professors</a>.</p><
 p>  Later that afternoon\, Townsend will also give the inaugural lecture i
 n a new series on the current landscape both for the humanities and for hu
 manities funding:</p><p><strong>What's Really Happening to the Humanities?
 </strong><br /><strong>(And What You Can Do About It)</strong></p><p>  4:0
 0 pm\, Bridgwaters Lounge\, Neal-Marshall Black Culture Center<br />  275 
 N. Eagleson Avenue\, Bloomington\, Indiana</p><p>  The decline in humaniti
 es majors is a familiar story\, but what explains it? And should that be t
 he principal measure of success for the field? Drawing on more than a deca
 de of research by the Humanities Indicators\, Townsend will offer a wide-r
 anging look at the state of the humanities\, examine the pressures shaping
  our future\, and suggest ways faculty and institutions might respond.</p>
 <p><strong>Robert Townsend</strong> oversees Humanities\, Arts\, and Cultu
 re programs and day-to-day work on the <a href="https://www.amacad.org/hum
 anities-indicators">Humanities Indicators</a> at the <a href="https://www.
 amacad.org/">American Academy of Arts and Sciences</a>. Prior to the Acade
 my\, he spent 24 years at the American Historical Association\, in positio
 ns ranging from editorial assistant to deputy director. He is the author o
 f <a href="https://press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/H/bo14312740.
 html"><em>History's Babel: Scholarship\, Professionalization\, and the His
 torical Enterprise in the United States\, 1880-1940</em></a> (University o
 f Chicago Press\, 2013) and author or co-author of over 200 articles on va
 rious aspects of history\, higher education\, and public humanities.</p><p
 >  These talks are presented by the College Arts + Humanities Institute wi
 th generous assistance from the College of Arts + Sciences and the Ruth N.
  Halls Humanities Fund. For more information\, please visit <a href="https
 ://cahi.indiana.edu">cahi.indiana.edu</a>. If need assistance or if you ha
 ve an accommodation request\, please email <a href="mailto:cahi@iu.edu">ca
 hi@iu.edu</a> or call 812-856-1169.</p>
