BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/cahi/event/2490143-the-current-landscape-of-the-humanities-with"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:The Current Landscape of the Humanities with Robert Townsend
DTSTART:20261204T210000Z
DTEND:20261204T220000Z
DTSTAMP:20261119T170422Z
UID:20261204T210000Z-2490143@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261204T210000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/130/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,600,600/33621_townsend-sq.rev.1795126053.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Full description:  This year\, CAHI has organized a series of 
 talks on the current landscape both for the humanities and for humanities 
 funding. On December 4\, Robert Townsend\, Program Director for Humanities
 \, Arts\, and Culture at the American Academy of Arts and Sciences\, will 
 give the inaugural lecture in the series.What's Really Happening to the Hu
 manities?(And What You Can Do About It)  4:00 pm\, Bridgwaters Lounge\, Ne
 al-Marshall Black Culture Center  275 N. Eagleson Avenue\, Bloomington\, I
 ndiana  The decline in humanities majors is a familiar story\, but what ex
 plains it? And should that be the principal measure of success for the fie
 ld? Drawing on more than a decade of research by the Humanities Indicators
 \, Townsend will offer a wide-ranging look at the state of the humanities\
 , examine the pressures shaping our future\, and suggest ways faculty and 
 institutions might respond.  Earlier in the day\, Townsend will give a tal
 k on artificial intelligence and the humanities\, co-sponsored by the Alli
 ance of Distinguished and Titled Professors (https://alliance.iu.edu/):AI 
 and Humanities Research: Risk\, Opportunity\, and Change  1:00 pm\, Bridgw
 aters Lounge\, Neal-Marshall Black Culture Center  275 N. Eagleson Avenue\
 , Bloomington\, Indiana  Drawing on recent experiments with AI for the Hum
 anities Indicators and a study of historians' adoption of earlier technolo
 gies\, Townsend will discuss AI's strengths and limitations in humanities 
 research\, how it might redraw the boundary between assistance and experti
 se in the field\, and why these experiments have nevertheless made him mor
 e optimistic about the future of the humanities.Robert Townsend oversees H
 umanities\, Arts\, and Culture programs and day-to-day work on the Humanit
 ies Indicators (https://www.amacad.org/humanities-indicators) at the Ameri
 can Academy of Arts and Sciences (https://www.amacad.org/). Prior to the A
 cademy\, he spent 24 years at the American Historical Association\, in pos
 itions ranging from editorial assistant to deputy director. He is the auth
 or of History's Babel: Scholarship\, Professionalization\, and the Histori
 cal Enterprise in the United States\, 1880-1940 (https://press.uchicago.ed
 u/ucp/books/book/chicago/H/bo14312740.html) (University of Chicago Press\,
  2013) and author or co-author of over 200 articles on various aspects of 
 history\, higher education\, and public humanities.Save these dates for mo
 re in the series:February 5: Claire Katz  President\, Board of Directors\,
  National Humanities AllianceMarch 24: Phillip Brian Harper  Vice Presiden
 t\, Programs and Higher Learning\, Mellon FoundationApril 8: Joy Connolly 
  President\, American Council of Learned Societies  These talks are presen
 ted by the College Arts + Humanities Institute with generous assistance fr
 om the College of Arts + Sciences and the Ruth N. Halls Humanities Fund. F
 or more information\, please visit cahi.indiana.edu (https://cahi.indiana.
 edu). If need assistance or if you have an accommodation request\, please 
 email cahi@iu.edu (mailto:mailto:cahi@iu.edu) or call 812-856-1169.  For t
 hose who cannot attend in person\, a Zoom option will be available. Please
  email cahi@iu.edu (mailto:cahi@iu.edu) for the Zoom link.
GEO:39.16829;-86.516866
LAST-MODIFIED:20261119T221505Z
LOCATION:Neal-Marshall Black Culture Center
URL:https://events.iu.edu/cahi/event/2490143-the-current-landscape-of-the-
 humanities-with
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Full description:</strong></p><p> 
  This year\, CAHI has organized a series of talks on the current landscape
  both for the humanities and for humanities funding. On December 4\, <a hr
 ef="https://www.amacad.org/person/robert-b-townsend" target="_blank" rel="
 noopener"><strong>Robert Townsend</strong></a>\, Program Director for Huma
 nities\, Arts\, and Culture at the American Academy of Arts and Sciences\,
  will give the inaugural lecture in the series.</p><p><strong>What's Reall
 y Happening to the Humanities?</strong><br /><strong>(And What You Can Do 
 About It)</strong></p><p>  4:00 pm\, Bridgwaters Lounge\, Neal-Marshall Bl
 ack Culture Center<br />  275 N. Eagleson Avenue\, Bloomington\, Indiana</
 p><p>  The decline in humanities majors is a familiar story\, but what exp
 lains it? And should that be the principal measure of success for the fiel
 d? Drawing on more than a decade of research by the Humanities Indicators\
 , Townsend will offer a wide-ranging look at the state of the humanities\,
  examine the pressures shaping our future\, and suggest ways faculty and i
 nstitutions might respond.</p><p>  Earlier in the day\, Townsend will give
  a talk on artificial intelligence and the humanities\, co-sponsored by th
 e <a href="https://alliance.iu.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Allian
 ce of Distinguished and Titled Professors</a>:</p><p><strong>AI and Humani
 ties Research: Risk\, Opportunity\, and Change</strong></p><p>  1:00 pm\, 
 Bridgwaters Lounge\, Neal-Marshall Black Culture Center<br />  275 N. Eagl
 eson Avenue\, Bloomington\, Indiana</p><p>  Drawing on recent experiments 
 with AI for the Humanities Indicators and a study of historians' adoption 
 of earlier technologies\, Townsend will discuss AI's strengths and limitat
 ions in humanities research\, how it might redraw the boundary between ass
 istance and expertise in the field\, and why these experiments have nevert
 heless made him more optimistic about the future of the humanities.</p><p>
 <strong>Robert Townsend</strong> oversees Humanities\, Arts\, and Culture 
 programs and day-to-day work on the <a href="https://www.amacad.org/humani
 ties-indicators">Humanities Indicators</a> at the <a href="https://www.ama
 cad.org/">American Academy of Arts and Sciences</a>. Prior to the Academy\
 , he spent 24 years at the American Historical Association\, in positions 
 ranging from editorial assistant to deputy director. He is the author of <
 em><a href="https://press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/H/bo14312740
 .html">History's Babel: Scholarship\, Professionalization\, and the Histor
 ical Enterprise in the United States\, 1880-1940</a></em> (University of C
 hicago Press\, 2013) and author or co-author of over 200 articles on vario
 us aspects of history\, higher education\, and public humanities.</p><p><s
 trong>Save these dates for more in the series:</strong></p><p><strong>Febr
 uary 5: Claire Katz</strong><br />  President\, Board of Directors\, Natio
 nal Humanities Alliance</p><p><strong>March 24: Phillip Brian Harper</stro
 ng><br />  Vice President\, Programs and Higher Learning\, Mellon Foundati
 on</p><p><strong>April 8: Joy Connolly</strong><br />  President\, America
 n Council of Learned Societies</p><p>  These talks are presented by the Co
 llege Arts + Humanities Institute with generous assistance from the Colleg
 e of Arts + Sciences and the Ruth N. Halls Humanities Fund. For more infor
 mation\, please visit <a href="https://cahi.indiana.edu">cahi.indiana.edu<
 /a>. If need assistance or if you have an accommodation request\, please e
 mail <a href="mailto:mailto:cahi@iu.edu">cahi@iu.edu</a> or call 812-856-1
 169.</p><p>  For those who cannot attend in person\, a Zoom option will be
  available. Please email <a href="mailto:cahi@iu.edu">cahi@iu.edu</a> for 
 the Zoom link.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  College Arts + Humanities Institute<br />\
 n  Gayle Karch Cook Center for Public Arts and Humanities<br />\n  Maxwell
  Hall\, 750 E. Kirkwood Avenue<br />\n  Indiana University\, Bloomington<b
 r />\n  <br />\n  web: <a href="https://cahi.indiana.edu">cahi.indiana.edu
 </a><br />\n  email: <a href="mailto:cahi@iu.edu">cahi@iu.edu</a>\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2490143
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/130/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,600\,600/33621_townsend-sq.rev.1795126053.jp
 g
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:Robert Townsend.
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Robert Townsend\, Director for Humanities\, Art
 s\, and Culture at the American Academy of Arts and Sciences\, will give t
 he inaugural lecture: "What's Really Happening to the Humanities? (and Wha
 t You Can Do About It)."\n</p>
