X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Cinema": "https://events.iu.edu/cinemaiub/event/2473309-teenage-sex-and-death-at-camp-miasma-additional", "Pillar Arts Community Calendar": "https://tockify.com/bloomington.arts.calendar/detail/2557/1796439600000"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 81": "https://events.iu.edu/cinemaiub/event/2473309-teenage-sex-and-death-at-camp-miasma-additional"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_81
X-SOURCE:IU Cinema, Pillar Arts Community Calendar
DTSTART:20261205T030000Z
DTEND:20261205T040000Z
LOCATION:IU CINEMA
GEO:39.168613;-86.517376
SUMMARY:Teenage Sex and Death at Camp Miasma | Additional Films and Guests
DESCRIPTION:Visionary director Jane Schoenbrun (I Saw the TV Glow) presen
 ts a new kind of horror remake in Teenage Sex and Death at Camp Miasma. B
 ursting with imagination and surreal wit\, their latest genre-bending won
 der affirms there's no such thing as a guilty pleasure. After years of sl
 apdash sequels and waning fandom\, the Camp Miasma slasher franchise is d
 ue for resurrection. And one young director (Hannah Einbinder) is more th
 an up to the task. But when she visits the original's star\, a now-reclus
 ive actress (Gillian Anderson)\, the two women fall into a blood-soaked w
 orld of desire\, fear\, delirium—under threat from the spear-wielding L
 ittle Death. Winner of the Queer Palm\, this transformative Cannes favori
 te leads its protagonists to true fulfillment\, encouraging us to ask how
  we can reach our own. [112 min\; horror\; English]
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Visionary director Jane Schoenbrun (<i>
 I Saw the TV Glow</i>) presents a new kind of horror remake in <i>Teenage
  Sex and Death at Camp Miasma</i>. Bursting with imagination and surreal 
 wit\, their latest genre-bending wonder affirms there's no such thing as 
 a guilty pleasure. After years of slapdash sequels and waning fandom\, th
 e <i>Camp Miasma</i> slasher franchise is due for resurrection. And one y
 oung director (Hannah Einbinder) is more than up to the task. But when sh
 e visits the original's star\, a now-reclusive actress (Gillian Anderson)
 \, the two women fall into a blood-soaked world of desire\, fear\, deliri
 um—under threat from the spear-wielding Little Death. Winner of the Que
 er Palm\, this transformative Cannes favorite leads its protagonists to t
 rue fulfillment\, encouraging us to ask how we can reach our own. [112 mi
 n\; horror\; English]</p>
