X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_11
SUMMARY:Grant Writing 101 and Public Funding for the Arts
DTSTART:20261211T150000Z
DTEND:20261211T170000Z
DTSTAMP:20261119T162753Z
UID:20261211T150000Z-2489981@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261211T150000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/234/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1500,1125/33613_IMG_6382_copy.rev.1795102334.jpg
CATEGORIES:Career development,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences,S
 tudent-run,Training, non-credit classes, and learning opportunities,Volunt
 eer and fundraising opportunities
DESCRIPTION:Have a big artistic vision\, passion project\, or community ar
 ts idea—and zero clue how to pay for it? Or maybe you need introductory 
 skills in understanding the application process for your future career?  F
 orget dry lectures and confusing jargon. This interactive session breaks d
 own the real-world basics of getting your creative work funded. We'll cove
 r:Decoding Public Funding: How city\, state\, and federal arts funders wor
 k—and how to find opportunities built for emerging artists and organizer
 s.  Making Data Your Superpower: How to gather and use compelling data to 
 back up your creative vision and make your grant applications stand out fr
 om the pile.  Zero experience is required!Whether you've never looked at a
  grant application in your life or just want to level up your resume\, thi
 s workshop builds the foundational skills you need to start applying with 
 confidence.  This workshop kicks off a three-part series designed to take 
 you through the entire funding journey\, continuing in Spring 2027. You ca
 n attend just one workshop or join us for all three!Workshop 1 (December 1
 1):Grant Writing 101 &amp\; Public Funding for the Arts  Public funding es
 sentials (city\, state\, federal) and using data to strengthen your applic
 ation.  Workshop 2 (Spring 2027):Foundation Funding for the Arts — Explo
 ring local and regional foundation opportunities\, building genuine funder
  relationships\, and crafting a standout story.  Workshop 3 (Spring 2027):
 Communicating Impact in the Arts and Culture Sector — Mastering the gran
 t cycle before and after you win the award\, including practical skills in
  evaluation planning\, documentation\, and impact reporting.
GEO:39.169144;-86.519739
LAST-MODIFIED:20261119T212753Z
LOCATION:SIDNEY AND LOIS ESKENAZI MUSEUM OF ART
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2489867-grant-writing-101-and-public
 -funding-for-the-arts
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><span style="caret-color: #000000\; color:
  #000000\; font-family: Aptos\, sans-serif\; font-size: 16px\; font-style:
  normal\; font-variant-caps: normal\; font-weight: 400\; letter-spacing: n
 ormal\; orphans: auto\; text-align: start\; text-indent: 0px\; text-transf
 orm: none\; white-space: normal\; widows: auto\; word-spacing: 0px\; -webk
 it-text-stroke-width: 0px\; background-color: #ffffff\; text-decoration: n
 one\; display: inline !important\; float: none\;">Have a big artistic visi
 on\, passion project\, or community arts idea—and zero clue how to pay f
 or it? Or maybe you need introductory skills in understanding the applicat
 ion process for your future career?</span></p><p style="font-weight: 400\;
 ">  Forget dry lectures and confusing jargon. This interactive session bre
 aks down the real-world basics of getting your creative work funded. We'll
  cover:</p><ul style="font-weight: 400\;"><li><strong>Decoding Public Fund
 ing:</strong> How city\, state\, and federal arts funders work—and how t
 o find opportunities built for emerging artists and organizers.  </li><li>
 <strong>Making Data Your Superpower:</strong> How to gather and use compel
 ling data to back up your creative vision and make your grant applications
  stand out from the pile.  </li></ul><p><strong>Zero experience is require
 d!</strong><span style="caret-color: #000000\; color: #000000\; font-famil
 y: Aptos\, sans-serif\; font-size: 16px\; font-style: normal\; font-varian
 t-caps: normal\; font-weight: 400\; letter-spacing: normal\; orphans: auto
 \; text-align: start\; text-indent: 0px\; text-transform: none\; white-spa
 ce: normal\; widows: auto\; word-spacing: 0px\; -webkit-text-stroke-width:
  0px\; background-color: #ffffff\; text-decoration: none\; display: inline
  !important\; float: none\;">Whether you've never looked at a grant applic
 ation in your life or just want to level up your resume\, this workshop bu
 ilds the foundational skills you need to start applying with confidence.</
 span></p><p style="font-weight: 400\;">  This workshop kicks off a three-p
 art series designed to take you through the entire funding journey\, conti
 nuing in <strong>Spring 2027</strong>. <strong>You can attend just one wor
 kshop or join us for all three!</strong></p><ul style="font-weight: 400\;"
 ><li><strong>Workshop 1 (December 11):</strong><em>Grant Writing 101 &amp\
 ; Public Funding for the Arts</em> — Public funding essentials (city\, s
 tate\, federal) and using data to strengthen your application.  </li><li><
 strong>Workshop 2 (Spring 2027):</strong><em>Foundation Funding for the Ar
 ts</em> — Exploring local and regional foundation opportunities\, buildi
 ng genuine funder relationships\, and crafting a standout story.  </li><li
 ><strong>Workshop 3 (Spring 2027):</strong><em>Communicating Impact in the
  Arts and Culture Sector</em> — Mastering the grant cycle before and aft
 er you win the award\, including practical skills in evaluation planning\,
  documentation\, and impact reporting.  </li></ul>
