BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 261": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2486396-student-showcase-winter-at-the-wylie-house"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_261
SUMMARY:Student Showcase: Winter at the Wylie House
DTSTART:20261211T213000Z
DTEND:20261211T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20261116T162717Z
UID:20261211T213000Z-2486396@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261211T213000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,3200,2559/33584_WylieHouseJonathanSchlesingerClass_202611
 05_20261105_20261112_035.rev.1794864426.jpg
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions,Community events,Student-run
DESCRIPTION:What can a ginkgo tree\, an orange\, a piano\, a bonnet\, or a
  doll teach us about early Bloomington? Find out how each is a clue to som
 ething bigger\, from the history of climate and social class to the work a
 nd lives of women.  Play games\, eat food\, and leave with a better unders
 tanding of Bloomington's past. Or…just party like it's 1835!Parking: Des
 ignated\, free Wylie House parking spots are located behind the museum and
  its adjacent education center. They are accessible via the alley. Free st
 reet parking is located directly in front of the museum on Second Street\,
  and additional free parking is available in neighborhood-zoned areas afte
 r 5:00 pm.Accessibility: The museum has accessible parking next to the mus
 eum's Morton C. Bradley\, Jr. Education Center\, with a path that leads to
  a ramp at the back door entrance of the museum. While the historic home's
  second floor is only accessible by staircase\, digital images and virtual
  tour options are provided for visitors who cannot visit the second floor.
  Please contact us in advance of your visit to discuss any specific needs.
GEO:39.161697;-86.5309
LAST-MODIFIED:20261116T212824Z
LOCATION:Wylie House Museum (307 E. Second Street)
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2486396-student-showcase-winter-
 at-the-wylie-house
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  What can a ginkgo tree\, an orange\, a p
 iano\, a bonnet\, or a doll teach us about early Bloomington? Find out how
  each is a clue to something bigger\, from the history of climate and soci
 al class to the work and lives of women.</p><p>  Play games\, eat food\, a
 nd leave with a better understanding of Bloomington's past. Or…just part
 y like it's 1835!</p><p><br /><strong>Parking</strong>: Designated\, free 
 Wylie House parking spots are located behind the museum and its adjacent e
 ducation center. They are accessible via the alley. Free street parking is
  located directly in front of the museum on Second Street\, and additional
  free parking is available in neighborhood-zoned areas after 5:00 pm.</p><
 p><strong>Accessibility</strong>: The museum has accessible parking next t
 o the museum's Morton C. Bradley\, Jr. Education Center\, with a path that
  leads to a ramp at the back door entrance of the museum. While the histor
 ic home's second floor is only accessible by staircase\, digital images an
 d virtual tour options are provided for visitors who cannot visit the seco
 nd floor. Please contact us in advance of your visit to discuss any specif
 ic needs.</p>
