BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 64": "https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2488540-environmental-justice-past-present-and-near-future"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_64
SUMMARY:Environmental Justice: Past\, Present\, and (Near) Future
DTSTART:20270211T220000Z
DTEND:20270211T230000Z
DTSTAMP:20261118T150453Z
UID:20270211T220000Z-2488540@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20270211T220000Z
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:This talk is part of Conservation Law Center's "Talking Conser
 vation" lecture series. For more information\, visit www.talkingconservati
 on.org.  Light refreshments will be served.Abstract:  When activists and s
 cholars speak of "environmental justice\," the first word people often hea
 r is race. And for good reason – early concerns for environmental justic
 e indeed focused on disproportionate impacts of environmental harm on comm
 unities of color and low-income populations. But over time\, environmental
  justice concerns have expanded well beyond race\, to include all people w
 ho may be disproportionately impacted by environmental harm and to conside
 r broader understandings of harm\, to include cumulative impacts and impac
 ts to cultural and subsistence resources. This presentation will explore w
 hat environmental justice can or should mean today\, against a backdrop of
  federal retrenchment\, yet full of hope for the future drawing upon lesso
 ns from the past.About Professor Villa:  Professor Cliff Villa teaches\, w
 rites\, and practices in the areas of constitutional rights and environmen
 tal law\, with particular expertise in environmental justice and disaster 
 response. On leave from UNM\, Prof. Villa served in the Biden administrati
 on as political leadership for the U.S. EPA Office of Land and Emergency M
 anagement. In that role\, based in Washington\, D.C.\, he provided policy 
 direction for national programs including Superfund cleanup\, Brownfields 
 revitalization\, and emergency response. Previously\, Villa served more th
 an 20 years as an EPA attorney in Washington\, D.C.\; Denver\, Colorado\; 
 and Seattle\, Washington. Within academia\, in addition to UNM\, Villa has
  taught at Columbia Law School and Seattle University. Among other publica
 tions\, Villa is the lead author of ENVIRONMENTAL JUSTICE: LAW\, POLICY &a
 mp\; REGULATION (3rd ed. 2020)\, and author of legal scholarship including
  Law and Lawyers in the Incident Command System (2013)\, Remaking Environm
 ental Justice (2020)\; Don't Blame the Flint River (2022)\, and Environmen
 tal Justice Beyond 2 ° (2024). Villa was born and raised in Albuquerque\,
  New Mexico\, with local roots tracing back to the Atrisco Land Grant of 1
 692.Register before 11:59pm on February 22\, 2027 to attend.&#160\;
GEO:39.165074;-86.526424
LAST-MODIFIED:20261120T160329Z
LOCATION:Lowell E. Baier Hall - Classroom - 120
URL:https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2488540-environmental-justice-past-
 present-and-near-future
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This talk is part of Conservation Law Ce
 nter's "Talking Conservation" lecture series. For more information\, visit
  www.talkingconservation.org.<br /><br />  Light refreshments will be serv
 ed.<br /><br /><span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>Abstract
 :</strong></span><br />  When activists and scholars speak of "environment
 al justice\," the first word people often hear is race. And for good reaso
 n – early concerns for environmental justice indeed focused on dispropor
 tionate impacts of environmental harm on communities of color and low-inco
 me populations. But over time\, environmental justice concerns have expand
 ed well beyond race\, to include all people who may be disproportionately 
 impacted by environmental harm and to consider broader understandings of h
 arm\, to include cumulative impacts and impacts to cultural and subsistenc
 e resources. This presentation will explore what environmental justice can
  or should mean today\, against a backdrop of federal retrenchment\, yet f
 ull of hope for the future drawing upon lessons from the past.<br /><br />
 <span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>About Professor Villa:<
 /strong></span><br />  Professor Cliff Villa teaches\, writes\, and practi
 ces in the areas of constitutional rights and environmental law\, with par
 ticular expertise in environmental justice and disaster response. On leave
  from UNM\, Prof. Villa served in the Biden administration as political le
 adership for the U.S. EPA Office of Land and Emergency Management. In that
  role\, based in Washington\, D.C.\, he provided policy direction for nati
 onal programs including Superfund cleanup\, Brownfields revitalization\, a
 nd emergency response. Previously\, Villa served more than 20 years as an 
 EPA attorney in Washington\, D.C.\; Denver\, Colorado\; and Seattle\, Wash
 ington. Within academia\, in addition to UNM\, Villa has taught at Columbi
 a Law School and Seattle University. Among other publications\, Villa is t
 he lead author of ENVIRONMENTAL JUSTICE: LAW\, POLICY &amp\; REGULATION (3
 rd ed. 2020)\, and author of legal scholarship including Law and Lawyers i
 n the Incident Command System (2013)\, Remaking Environmental Justice (202
 0)\; Don't Blame the Flint River (2022)\, and Environmental Justice Beyond
  2 ° (2024). Villa was born and raised in Albuquerque\, New Mexico\, with
  local roots tracing back to the Atrisco Land Grant of 1692.</p><p><strong
 >Register before 11:59pm on February 22\, 2027 to attend.&#160\;</strong><
 /p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  <strong>Katie Beck<br /></strong>Director 
 of Advancement<br />\n  Conservation Law Center<br />\n  admin@conservatio
 nlawcenter.org\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2488540
X-LIVEWHALE-RSVP:1
X-LIVEWHALE-TAGS:American Constitution Society|Conservation Law Clinic|Env
 ironmental Law Society|Health Law Student Association|Jewish Law Student A
 ssociation|Public Interest Law Foundation
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Maurer School of Law
END:VEVENT
